{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1508 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "7dec899268f88e9c80e25a6f9c1ae8fe1ed90bc89bd76b56850a7648928aa6d9" :edb/db-before-t 1507 :edb/db-after-t 1508 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534820 50 #inst "2026-10-08T08:01:37.444Z" 13194139534820 true] [17592186166639 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/8a4c773c2ccc0bbab1ebb7126b781504549a46b6f5991753866eff98f3c8e03d.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- $y' = y^2(y-1)$\n\n- $y' = y(y-1)(y-4)$\n\n- $y' = -(y-1)^2(y-4)$" 13194139534820 true] [17592186166639 1100 "Which of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/ebef86d8bf088f7bfceaaed94999c6cd.png)\n\n1. $y' = y^2(y-1)$\n2. $y' = y(y-1)(y-4)$\n3. $y' = -(y-1)^2(y-4)$" 13194139534820 false] [17592186166639 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/ee21aabb9f81aed281951e5ff9420c5f2985bfbc192791d8d2bbd1f1c87de156.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y = 1$ and $y = 4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation $y' = y^2(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=1.$\n\n- The equation $y' = -y(y-1)(y-4)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=4,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $y=0,$ which is not behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation $y' =-(y-1)^2(y-4)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534820 true] [17592186166639 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/fad2dd38b97e530e13888329b587e157.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y = 1$ and $y = 4$, as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation $y' = y^2(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=1$.\n- The equation $y' = -y(y-1)(y-4)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=4$, but it also evaluates to zero when $y=0$, which is not behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n- The equation $y' =-(y-1)^2(y-4)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=1$ and $y=4$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534820 false] [17592186166640 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/998b30d8fb1df0c32a6ec63881ea247702a8e3888e7cd63ca371bc7dfe40ee2d.png)\n\n \n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n- $y = -y^2(y+4)$\n\n- $y = y(y-4)^2$\n\n- $y = y(y+4)$" 13194139534820 true] [17592186166640 1100 "The diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/86ab6710d8a01fe1648d5b39a316ed5b.png)\n\n1. $y = -y^2(y+4)$\n2. $y = y(y-4)^2$\n3. $y = y(y+4)$" 13194139534820 false] [17592186166640 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/fb7c6c77767368871d3b39991093906f6a4a0f94e2a83075570e22b3f0e87a78.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-4$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-{y}^{2}(y+4) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =y(y+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-4$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y <                                    -4$ | $-4 <                                    y <                                    0$ | $y > 0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\\nearrow$ | $\\searrow$ | $\\searrow$ |\n| Sign | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\ny' = -y^2(y+4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y' = -y^2(y+4).$" 13194139534820 true] [17592186166640 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/d4a4ac0f384c15b61fcaed3773ab2b76.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-4$ and $y=0$. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.Of the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n$y^{′}|=-y^{2}(y + 4); y^{′}|= y(y + 4)$\n- Second, the lines $y=-4$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\nRegion$y < -4$$-4 < y < 0$$y > 0$Slope$\\nearrow$$\\searrow$$\\searrow$Sign$\\color{blue}+$$\\color{red}-$$\\color{red}-$\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n$y' = -y^2(y+4)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$$\ny' = -y^2(y+4)\n$$" 13194139534820 false] [17592186166641 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/f93764bdd958d41e90e09e8be2f52754835c67bc5a2ff9f6186454ebbd245232.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{y}{y+1}$\n\n- $y'= \\dfrac1{y^2+y}$\n\n- $y' = \\dfrac{2}{y+1}$" 13194139534820 true] [17592186166641 1100 "Which of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/0ef8e5a6169e508db61f76dfc5ec0e3a.png)\n\n1. $y' = \\dfrac{y}{y+1}$\n2. $y'= \\dfrac1{y^2+y}$\n3. $y' = \\dfrac{2}{y+1}$" 13194139534820 false] [17592186166641 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/8820a0d0a4e0fc9d0089dea3c34a67bfaf619edcb7e0552d14a11b42a4cd20e5.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to -1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to\\pm\\infty.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $y > -1$ and negative for $y < -1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{y}{y+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y}{y+1} = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(1+\\dfrac1y\\right)}\\to 1 \\quad\\text{as} \\quad y\\to \\pm\\infty.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to\\pm\\infty.$\n\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac1{y^2+y}= \\dfrac1{y(y+1)}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become vertical for *both* $y \\to -1$ *and* $y \\to 0,$ \nwhich is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{2}{y+1}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that $y'\\to 0$ as $y\\to\\pm\\infty,$ and $y'\\to \\infty$ as $y\\to -1.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $y > -1$ and negative for $y < -1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534820 true] [17592186166641 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/e919936fc92a4b4110ef32705d009b5c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to -1$.\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to\\pm\\infty$.\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive for $y > -1$ and negative for $y < -1$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{y}{y+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n$\\dfrac{y}{y+1} = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(1+\\dfrac1y\\right)}\\to 1 \\quad\\text{as} \\quad y\\to \\pm\\infty$.\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to\\pm\\infty$.\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac1{y^2+y}= \\dfrac1{y(y+1)}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become vertical for *both* $y \\to -1$ *and* $y \\to 0$,\nwhich is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n- The equation $y' = \\dfrac{2}{y+1}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that $y'\\to 0$ as $y\\to\\pm\\infty$, and $y'\\to \\infty$ as $y\\to -1$. Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for $y > -1$ and negative for $y < -1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534820 false] [17592186166643 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/b064d9170c89eda881a1247b14fa295bae6b78d794ad7737aa86872fb5277af1.png)\n\n \n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n- $y' = y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y$\n\n- $y' = y^2 - 2y$\n\n- $y' = y^3 + y^2$" 13194139534820 true] [17592186166643 1100 "The diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/68e980c93a3dde61e9b3777f266b0be6.png)\n\n1. $y' = y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y$\n2. $y' = y^2 - 2y$\n3. $y' = y^3 + y^2$" 13194139534820 false] [17592186166643 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/96fa01db29c0effec6c81996cfcaad1d46ffa71f2f1b2b3da0a48dc39bfb0d26.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y = 0$ and $y = 2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\ny'= y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y = y(y^2 - 4y + 4) = y(y-2)^2\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\ny'=  y^2 - 2y = y(y-2).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y = 0$ and $y = 2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y <        0$ | $0 <          y <          2$ | $y > 2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $\\searrow$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n| Sign | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\ny ' = y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y =y(y-2)^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y' =y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y.$" 13194139534820 true] [17592186166643 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/ba11bc7d92bbbefc467705ceeece8cea.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y = 0$ and $y = 2$. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.Of the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n$y'= y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y = y(y^2 - 4y + 4) = y(y-2)^2$\nand\n$y'= y^2 - 2y = y(y-2)$.\n- Second, the lines $y = 0$ and $y = 2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\nRegion$y < 0$$0 < y < 2$$y > 2$Slope$\\searrow$$\\nearrow$$\\nearrow$Sign$\\color{red}-$$\\color{blue}+$$\\color{blue}+$\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n$y ' = y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y =y(y-2)^2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is\n\n$$\ny' =y^3 - 4y^2 + 4y\n$$" 13194139534820 false] [17592186166786 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312188 13194139534820 true] [17592186166786 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312195 13194139534820 true] [17592186166786 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312202 13194139534820 true] [17592186166786 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312279 13194139534820 true] [17592186166786 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312286 13194139534820 true] [17592186166787 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312209 13194139534820 true] [17592186166787 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312216 13194139534820 true] [17592186166787 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312223 13194139534820 true] [17592186166787 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312293 13194139534820 true] [17592186166788 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312230 13194139534820 true] [17592186166788 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312237 13194139534820 true] [17592186166788 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312258 13194139534820 true] [17592186166788 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312265 13194139534820 true] [17592186166790 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312244 13194139534820 true] [17592186166790 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312251 13194139534820 true] [17592186166790 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312272 13194139534820 true] [17592186166790 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312300 13194139534820 true] [17592186166790 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312307 13194139534820 true] [17592186167015 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534820 true] [17592186167015 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/b7feb77818b19ab8b61471df9057a15fb3c39d6babc4aeee56a73e1d3c3756cb.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139534820 true] [17592186167015 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/q-115466.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139534820 false] [17592186167015 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/1e37d101f131279fc198fd97f03e6a35e15bc2d62622e1730aadbc316dc8d0bf.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{2}y(y+2)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}y(y+2)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}(y+2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-2$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-2$ | $-2<y<0$ | $y>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}y(y+2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}y(y+2)^{2}.$" 13194139534820 true] [17592186167017 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534820 true] [17592186167017 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/1b608fabe99c6062f9d9f04cc7f3b2933e453812ed4ce9783908666c71c1c74b.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{y-1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}$" 13194139534820 true] [17592186167017 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3220/Source/Images/q-115479.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n1. $y^{′} = \\frac{y}{y - 1}$\n2. $y^{′} = \\frac{1}{y}$\n3. $y^{′} = \\frac{1}{y^{2}}$" 13194139534820 false] [17592186167017 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/7bb52825944a7515a6281a88b5bc740cf6d097380fa13fe7e00efc5e499d49f8.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive everywhere.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{y-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{y}{y-1}=\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{y})}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are negative for $y<0.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to 0.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for all $y\\ne 0.$" 13194139534820 true] [17592186312188 1017 #edb/ref 17592186312189 13194139534820 true] [17592186312188 1089 "q-115876" 13194139534820 true] [17592186312188 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534820 true] [17592186312188 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/bedcab6fac21c2c178604e94f04f31bf1f423562fd653fd18ae5e0ea949e38dc.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by ${y}^{′}=(y-a)(y-b).$ Find the value of $a+b.$" 13194139534820 true] [17592186312188 1113 #uuid "7474d0ef-0afb-5a88-8581-bff8157f9b7a" 13194139534820 true] [17592186312188 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040443/assets/84b459d5682327d618116cd5d3ef331263548c754d2d8e0759e6fcb084fd466c.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-2$ and $y=-4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y+2)(y+4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=-2,b=-4,$ and $a+b=-6.$" 13194139534820 true] [17592186312189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312190 13194139534820 true] [17592186312189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312191 13194139534820 true] [17592186312189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312192 13194139534820 true] [17592186312189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312193 13194139534820 true] [17592186312189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312194 13194139534820 true] [17592186312189 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534820 true] [17592186312189 1041 "selection" 13194139534820 true] [17592186312189 1104 #edb/ref 17592186312194 13194139534820 true] [17592186312189 1110 #uuid "d41ad310-ab0d-515c-8afc-c90bf4ea5370" 13194139534820 true] [17592186312190 1034 "2" 13194139534820 true] [17592186312190 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534820 true] [17592186312190 1111 #uuid "a8e6f230-2c29-5579-86d3-60ce540b7465" 13194139534820 true] [17592186312191 1034 "6" 13194139534820 true] [1759218631