tice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-1$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(y+1)(y-2)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(y+1)(y-2) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =y(y+1)(2-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can dismiss the equation ${y}^{′}=y(y+1)(2-y).$ Although the right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=2,$ it also evaluates to zero when $y=0,$ which is behavior not exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=-1$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-1$ | $-1<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-2)^{2}.$" 13194139534820 true] [17592186312224 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312225 13194139534820 true] [17592186312224 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312226 13194139534820 true] [17592186312224 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312227 13194139534820 true] [17592186312224 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312228 13194139534820 true] [17592186312224 1000 #edb/ref 17592186312229 13194139534820 true] [17592186312224 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534820 true] [17592186312224 1041 "selection" 13194139534820 true] [17592186312224 1104 #edb/ref 17592186312228 13194139534820 true] [17592186312224 1110 #uuid "a71dd1b3-b660-5833-8400-e07f3e2cdc68" 13194139534820 true] [17592186312225 1034 "{y}^{′}=(y-1)^{2}" 13194139534820 true] [17592186312225 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534820 true] [175921863